     1	# sdd-dispatch
     2	
     3	Standalone Claude Code plugin for **subagent-driven development via external CLIs**
     4	(codex / opencode / agy): token-efficient plan execution plus the empirically-verified
     5	knowledge base that makes it safe.
     6	
     7	Everything is self-contained — skills reference plugin files via `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}`;
     8	no dependence on global CLAUDE.md tables or machine-specific paths.
     9	
    10	## Install
    11	
    12	```
    13	/plugin marketplace add /path/to/sdd-dispatch-plugin
    14	/plugin install sdd-dispatch@sdd-dispatch-marketplace
    15	```
    16	
    17	Requires: the `superpowers` plugin (this wraps its subagent-driven-development skill),
    18	and whichever dispatch CLIs you use on PATH (`codex`, `opencode`, `agy` — each
    19	authenticated once interactively).
    20	
    21	## Skills
    22	
    23	| Skill | Purpose |
    24	| --- | --- |
    25	| `sdd` | Execute an implementation plan: wraps superpowers:subagent-driven-development, replacing its Agent-tool dispatch with external-CLI dispatch (contracts as files, resume-based Q&A/fixes, enforced read-only reviewers, controller commits, liveness protocol, risk-scaled gate) |
    26	| `sdd-dispatch-verify` | Re-run the CLI probe suite when versions bump or models release; updates the living references and appends to the verification log |
    27	
    28	## Layout
    29	
    30	```
    31	skills/sdd/                      # plan-execution skill (the entry point)
    32	skills/sdd-dispatch-verify/      # CLI re-verification skill
    33	contracts/                       # implementer + task-reviewer operating contracts
    34	                                 #   (copied once per session into the SDD workspace)
    35	references/
    36	  sdd-external-dispatch.md       # the playbook: role→lane mapping, token-efficiency rules
    37	  dispatch-reference.md          # verified per-CLI behavior, gotchas, liveness protocol
    38	  model-catalog.md               # role→tier→model policy table + provider inventories
    39	  verification-protocol.md       # the repeatable probe suite (P1–P12)
    40	  verification-log.md            # append-only verdict history (never rewrite entries)
    41	```
    42	
    43	## Living-document rules
    44	
    45	Facts in `references/` are version-stamped and empirically probed — vendor claims enter
    46	as *reported* until verified. Re-verification triggers: CLI version bump, model release,
    47	a gotcha firing (or failing to fire), or quarterly. Run the `sdd-dispatch-verify` skill;
    48	it appends to the log, updates the references, and bumps the plugin version.
    49	
    50	Origin: seeded 2026-07-22 from a full three-CLI verification round
    51	(codex 0.144.3, opencode 1.17.18, agy 1.1.4).
     1	---
     2	name: sdd
     3	description: Execute an implementation plan via subagent-driven development with external-CLI dispatch (codex/opencode/agy). Use whenever executing a written plan with SDD — wraps superpowers:subagent-driven-development and applies the external-dispatch optimizations mechanically. Triggers: "run this plan with SDD", "/sdd", "execute the plan via subagents", the Standard Delivery Flow reaching its execute step.
     4	---
     5	
     6	# SDD with External-CLI Dispatch
     7	
     8	This skill wraps **superpowers:subagent-driven-development**: that skill's process governs
     9	(per-task loop, task-brief/review-package scripts, statuses, two-verdict reviews, fix
    10	loops, ledger, pre-flight scan, final review). This skill replaces its **dispatch
    11	mechanism** with external CLIs.
    12	
    13	Plugin references (read when a step needs detail):
    14	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/sdd-external-dispatch.md` — the playbook + rationale
    15	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/dispatch-reference.md` — verified per-CLI behavior, gotchas, liveness protocol
    16	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/model-catalog.md` — role→tier→model table (the authority for model choice)
    17	
    18	## Step 0 — Setup (once per session, before Task 1)
    19	
    20	1. Invoke `superpowers:subagent-driven-development` and follow its process EXCEPT the
    21	   dispatch steps, which this skill overrides.
    22	2. Run its `scripts/sdd-workspace`; copy the operating contracts into it:
    23	   `cp "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/contracts/"*.md "$WORKSPACE"/`
    24	3. Check the ledger (`$WORKSPACE/progress.md`) — never re-dispatch a completed task.
    25	4. Read the role→model table from `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/model-catalog.md`
    26	   and note the routing lever in effect: silent → "floor it" (cheapest model clearing
    27	   each bar); "play it safe" → one tier up on implementers; "via agy" / "delegate
    28	   mechanical to opencode" / "all Claude" reroute lanes. **"all Claude" = use the stock
    29	   skill's Agent-tool dispatch and skip this skill's overrides.**
    30	
    31	## Dispatch overrides (replace the skill's "dispatch subagent" steps)
    32	
    33	**Implementer** (default lane codex; model from the catalog table — ALWAYS explicit):
    34	```bash
    35	BASE=$(git rev-parse HEAD)   # record BEFORE dispatch; never HEAD~1 later
    36	LOG=$WORKSPACE/task-N.log
    37	timeout --kill-after=30s <backstop≈4-5x estimate> \
    38	codex exec -m <model> -C <repo> -s workspace-write -c approval_policy="never" \
    39	  -c model_reasoning_effort=<effort> --skip-git-repo-check -o $WORKSPACE/task-N-status.md \
    40	  "Read $WORKSPACE/implementer-contract.md — your operating contract. \
    41	   Read $WORKSPACE/task-N-brief.md — your complete requirements. \
    42	   Scene: <one line: where this task fits>. \
    43	   Interfaces from prior tasks: <lines, or 'none'>. \
    44	   Write your full report to $WORKSPACE/task-N-report.md. Begin." \
    45	  > "$LOG" 2>&1 &
    46	```
    47	Run in background; apply the **liveness protocol** (stall evidence is the only kill
    48	criterion, never elapsed time — full rules in dispatch-reference.md).
    49	
    50	**Self-reaping dispatch (harness background tasks — the standard shape):** wrap the CLI
    51	and its stall watch in ONE background command, so stalls are killed at threshold without
    52	controller turns, the controller stays free to answer the user, and the completion
    53	notification means "finished or stall-killed":
    54	```bash
    55	<cli dispatch> > "$LOG" 2>&1 &
    56	CLI=$!
    57	while kill -0 $CLI 2>/dev/null; do
    58	  age=$(( $(date +%s) - $(stat -c %Y "$LOG") ))
    59	  [ $age -gt <stall-threshold, 300 for codex/opencode> ] && { kill $CLI; echo "STALL-KILLED after ${age}s log silence"; break; }
    60	  sleep 10
    61	done
    62	wait $CLI 2>/dev/null; echo "cli exit=$?"
    63	```
    64	(For agy, watch process existence + `--print-timeout`, not log age — it buffers and a
    65	log-age watch WOULD kill healthy runs.)
    66	**Threshold scales with reasoning effort** — silence is only stall evidence relative to
    67	the model's expected cadence: 300s at low/medium effort; 600–900s for high/xhigh-effort
    68	dispatches, whose single thinking steps can legitimately exceed 5 min of no output. And a
    69	stall-kill is always a checkpoint, never a loss: session id + working tree survive; if a
    70	killed run turns out to have been healthy (resume shows real progress), resume it and
    71	raise the threshold — never lower it mid-plan on suspicion alone.
    72	Never dispatch foreground for anything longer than a sub-minute probe: a foreground call
    73	blocks the controller from answering the user AND disables the stall rule — the only kill
    74	left is the coarse backstop.
    75	
    76	**Task reviewer** — same shape but **`-s read-only`** (enforced), reviewer-tier model,
    77	prompt names: `task-reviewer-contract.md`, the brief, the report, the review-package
    78	path, and the global constraints copied VERBATIM from the plan. Phrase the restriction
    79	"review only, change nothing in the repo; writing your review file is allowed" — a bare
    80	"modify nothing" makes obedient models skip the review file and answer inline (fine, but
    81	then the verdicts live only in the log tail).
    82	
    83	**Fix / NEEDS_CONTEXT** — never a cold dispatch: resume the implementer's session
    84	(`codex exec resume --last "<answers or findings list>"`; opencode `run -s <id>`;
    85	agy `--conversation <id>`), then re-review.
    86	
    87	## Flavour choice (be explicit which "dispatch" you mean)
    88	
    89	- **Inline** (no dispatch): task below the orchestration floor — a single-file mechanical
    90	  fix the controller finishes in <~2k tokens. Batch several such tasks into one
    91	  ext-dispatch instead of paying per-task cold starts.
    92	- **Ext-dispatch** (this skill's default): Bash → external CLI, per the templates above.
    93	- **Supervised ext-dispatch** (long plans, > ~8 tasks): spawn ONE cheap Claude subagent
    94	  (haiku/sonnet, Agent tool) per task-cycle to run the ext-dispatch templates, liveness,
    95	  mechanical gate, and reviewer dispatch, returning a single report with evidence paths.
    96	  Adjudication and commits STILL happen here in the main thread.
    97	- **Sub-dispatch** (Claude subagent does the work itself) = the "all Claude" lever.
    98	Economics table: references/sdd-external-dispatch.md "Dispatch flavours & economics".
    99	
   100	## Controller rules (the hard gate — never offloaded)
   101	
   102	- Read back ONLY the status block, `git diff --stat`, and reviewer verdicts. Full diff
   103	  only on findings, ⚠️ items, critical paths, or stat/report disagreement. Never let CLI
   104	  stdout or full logs into context.
   105	- On DONE: re-run the covering tests yourself (output to file, read tail), then
   106	  **you commit** (implementers must not; codex cannot). Then `review-package $BASE HEAD`.
   107	- Ledger line per completed task: `Task N: complete (commits <b7>..<h7>, review clean, session <id>)`.
   108	- Adjudication stays yours: statuses, ⚠️ resolution, plan contradictions → batched to the
   109	  human. Never parallel implementers. Never re-dispatch a ledgered task.
   110	- Final whole-branch review: most-capable tier, `review-package MERGE_BASE HEAD` +
   111	  Minor-findings list; ONE consolidated fix dispatch if findings.
   112	
   113	## Gotcha quick-list (full detail: references/dispatch-reference.md)
   114	
   115	`< /dev/null` on codex/agy always · agy `-p "<PROMPT>"` LAST · opencode prompt positional
   116	(`-p`=password) · agy buffers (judge liveness by process, not log growth; brain-file sweep
   117	if stdout empty) · only codex is sandboxed — clean tree before, diff after, on agy/opencode
   118	· user asks "still running?" → check evidence immediately, never assert from belief
   119	· dispatch with the self-reaping wrapper (auto-kills on stall, notification == outcome);
   120	kill by recorded pid, never pkill-by-pattern from a dispatching shell; foreground only
   121	for sub-minute probes
   122	· opencode can hang at startup with a forever-0-byte log — stall rules apply from byte 0;
   123	after 2 consecutive channel stalls on a small fix, do it inline
   124	· opencode session ids come from `opencode session list`, not the run log.
     1	---
     2	name: sdd-dispatch-verify
     3	description: Re-verify SDD dispatch CLI behavior (codex, opencode, agy) against this plugin's living knowledge base. Use when a CLI version bump is observed, a vendor releases new models, a dispatch behaves contrary to documented gotchas, or the user asks to "verify/test the dispatch CLIs", "re-run the CLI probes", or "check if the SDD reference is current".
     4	---
     5	
     6	# SDD Dispatch Verification
     7	
     8	Knowledge base (this plugin's `references/` directory — the living source of truth):
     9	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/verification-protocol.md` — the probe suite P1–P12
    10	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/dispatch-reference.md` — per-CLI facts + version stamps
    11	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/model-catalog.md` — model inventories + tiering
    12	- `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/references/verification-log.md` — append-only verdict history
    13	
    14	## Process
    15	
    16	1. **Read first**: the verification protocol (probe suite and ground rules) and the
    17	   current dispatch-reference version stamps. Determine which CLI(s) need a round:
    18	   compare `codex --version` / `opencode --version` / `agy --version` against the stamps,
    19	   and `opencode models` / `agy models` against the model catalog.
    20	
    21	2. **Run the probe suite** for each affected CLI, in the session scratchpad directory.
    22	   Ground rules that must not be skipped:
    23	   - never mask exit codes with pipes (use `${PIPESTATUS[0]}` or unpiped `$?`)
    24	   - verify side effects on disk, never from agent prose
    25	   - bound every probe with `timeout`; treat 124/143 as "hangs"
    26	   - use each CLI's cheapest model for probes
    27	
    28	3. **Record**: append a dated verdict-matrix entry to `references/verification-log.md`
    29	   (Confirmed / Refuted / Refined / New, with one-line raw evidence). Never edit prior
    30	   entries — contradictions date behavior changes.
    31	
    32	4. **Update the living docs**: `references/dispatch-reference.md` (facts + version
    33	   stamps), `references/model-catalog.md` (inventories, verified-status, watch list,
    34	   release history). If findings change how the `sdd` skill should dispatch, update
    35	   `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/skills/sdd/SKILL.md` and the contracts in
    36	   `${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}/contracts/` in the same round.
    37	
    38	5. **Clean up** all probe artifacts: scratchpad files, throwaway git repos, and any
    39	   `/tmp` files created by the sandbox-escape probe (P7).
    40	
    41	6. **Commit** in the plugin's own repo (`git -C "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT}" …` if working the
    42	   installed copy is the source checkout; otherwise commit in the source checkout and
    43	   reinstall). Bump the plugin version in `.claude-plugin/plugin.json` for behavior-fact
    44	   changes. Message like:
    45	   `verify: round YYYY-MM-DD — <cli> <old>→<new>, <headline finding>`.
    46	
    47	## Cautions
    48	
    49	- agy's permission model flipped completely between patch releases (1.1.1 → 1.1.4);
    50	  never assume any CLI's permission/sandbox behavior survived a version bump.
    51	- agy dispatches must put `-p "<PROMPT>"` last; codex and agy need `< /dev/null`.
    52	- These probes execute real dispatches that can WRITE (agy/opencode have no sandbox):
    53	  run only in the scratchpad, on a clean tree, and diff after.
     1	# Implementer Operating Contract (external-CLI edition)
     2	
     3	You are implementing one task from a larger plan. Your dispatch message names your task
     4	brief file, report file, and any interfaces from earlier tasks. This contract is how you
     5	operate. Adapted from superpowers:subagent-driven-development for external CLI dispatch.
     6	
     7	## Before you begin
     8	
     9	Read the task brief file named in your dispatch — it is your complete requirements, with
    10	the exact values to use verbatim. If anything is unclear — requirements, acceptance
    11	criteria, approach, dependencies — **stop and ask**: write your questions in your final
    12	message with status NEEDS_CONTEXT. Do not guess. The controller will answer by resuming
    13	this session.
    14	
    15	## Your job
    16	
    17	1. Implement exactly what the brief specifies — nothing more, nothing less.
    18	2. Write tests (TDD if the brief says so). While iterating, run the focused test for what
    19	   you're changing; run the full suite once at the end, not after every edit.
    20	3. Verify the implementation works.
    21	4. Self-review (below), fixing what you find.
    22	5. Write your report and finish. **Do NOT run `git commit`, `git add`, or mutate git
    23	   state in any way — the controller commits after gating your work.** (In the codex
    24	   sandbox `.git` is read-only and the commit would fail anyway.)
    25	
    26	## Code organization
    27	
    28	- Follow the file structure the plan defines; one clear responsibility per file.
    29	- In existing codebases follow established patterns. Improve code you touch the way a
    30	  good developer would; don't restructure outside your task.
    31	- If a file you're creating grows beyond the plan's intent, don't split it on your own —
    32	  finish and report DONE_WITH_CONCERNS naming it.
    33	
    34	## When you're in over your head
    35	
    36	It is always OK to stop and say this is too hard. Bad work is worse than no work; you
    37	will not be penalized for escalating. STOP and report BLOCKED when the task needs
    38	architectural decisions with multiple valid approaches, you can't reach clarity on code
    39	you need to understand, or you're reading file after file without progress. Say what
    40	you're stuck on, what you tried, and what help you need.
    41	
    42	## Self-review before reporting
    43	
    44	- **Completeness:** every requirement implemented? edge cases handled?
    45	- **Quality:** best work? names match what things do? clean and maintainable?
    46	- **Discipline:** only what was requested? existing patterns followed?
    47	- **Testing:** tests verify real behavior (not mocks)? TDD evidence if required?
    48	  test output pristine (no stray warnings/noise)?
    49	
    50	Fix what you find now, before reporting.
    51	
    52	## Report
    53	
    54	Write the FULL report to the report file named in your dispatch:
    55	- What you implemented (or attempted, if blocked)
    56	- What you tested, commands run, and results
    57	- TDD evidence if required (RED: command + failing output + why expected; GREEN: command + passing output)
    58	- Files changed
    59	- Self-review findings
    60	- Issues or concerns
    61	
    62	Then your **final message** is ONLY this status block (≤15 lines — detail lives in the
    63	report file):
    64	
    65	```
    66	STATUS: DONE | DONE_WITH_CONCERNS | BLOCKED | NEEDS_CONTEXT
    67	FILES: <changed files, one line>
    68	TESTS: <one line, e.g. "14/14 passing, output pristine">
    69	CONCERNS: <one line each, or "none">
    70	REPORT: <report file path>
    71	```
    72	
    73	If BLOCKED or NEEDS_CONTEXT, put the specifics in the final message itself — the
    74	controller acts on it directly. Never silently produce work you're unsure about.
    75	
    76	## After review findings (resumed session)
    77	
    78	If the controller resumes this session with reviewer findings: fix them, re-run the tests
    79	covering the amended code, APPEND the fix report (fixes made, commands, output) to the
    80	same report file, and reply with a fresh status block.
     1	# Task Reviewer Operating Contract (external-CLI edition)
     2	
     3	You are reviewing one task's implementation: first whether it matches its requirements,
     4	then whether it is well-built. This is a task-scoped gate, not a merge review — a broad
     5	whole-branch review happens separately. Your dispatch message names the task brief file,
     6	the implementer's report file, the diff (review-package) file, and the global constraints
     7	that bind this task. Adapted from superpowers:subagent-driven-development for external
     8	CLI dispatch (run read-only where the CLI supports it).
     9	
    10	## Inputs and method
    11	
    12	- **Brief file** = what was requested. The global-constraints block in your dispatch is
    13	  binding — exact values, formats, and relationships the spec demands.
    14	- **Report file** = what the implementer CLAIMS. Treat every claim — including design
    15	  rationales like "kept it simple deliberately" or "left it per YAGNI" — as unverified.
    16	  Judge the code on its merits; a stated rationale never downgrades a finding.
    17	- **Diff file** = your view of the change: commit list (or stat header), stat summary,
    18	  and full diff with extended context. Read it once. The diff's context lines ARE the
    19	  changed files — do not read a changed file separately unless a hunk you must judge is
    20	  cut off mid-function (and say so). Do not re-run git commands; do not crawl the broader
    21	  codebase. Inspect code outside the diff only to evaluate a concrete risk you can name —
    22	  one focused check per named risk, and name both the risk and what you checked.
    23	  Cross-cutting changes (lock ordering, API contracts, shared mutable state) are
    24	  legitimate named risks: checking call sites is the right method.
    25	- **Read-only:** do not mutate the working tree, index, or any git state.
    26	- **Tests:** the implementer already ran them and the report carries the evidence — do
    27	  not re-run the suite to confirm it. Run a test only when reading the code raises a
    28	  specific doubt no existing run answers, and then a focused test, never a package-wide
    29	  suite. If heavy validation seems warranted, recommend it instead of running it.
    30	  Warnings or noise in the reported test output are findings — output should be pristine.
    31	
    32	## Part 1 — Spec compliance
    33	
    34	Compare the diff against the brief:
    35	- **Missing:** requirements skipped, missed, or claimed without implementing
    36	- **Extra:** features not requested, over-engineering
    37	- **Misunderstood:** right feature built wrong, wrong problem solved
    38	
    39	If a requirement cannot be verified from this diff alone (lives in unchanged code, spans
    40	tasks), report it as a ⚠️ item rather than broadening your search.
    41	
    42	## Part 2 — Code quality
    43	
    44	- Clean separation of concerns; proper error handling; DRY without premature abstraction;
    45	  edge cases handled
    46	- Tests verify real behavior, not mocks; the task's edge cases covered
    47	- Each file one clear responsibility; follows the plan's file structure; did THIS change
    48	  create oversized files (don't flag pre-existing size)
    49	
    50	Every finding and every check you'd otherwise answer with a bare "yes" gets a file:line
    51	reference.
    52	
    53	## Calibration
    54	
    55	Not everything is Critical. **Important** = this task cannot be trusted until fixed:
    56	incorrect/fragile behavior, a missed requirement, or merge-blocking maintainability
    57	damage (verbatim duplicated logic, swallowed errors, tests that assert nothing).
    58	"Coverage could be broader" and polish are **Minor**. If the plan/brief explicitly
    59	mandates something this rubric calls a defect, that IS a finding — Important, labeled
    60	**plan-mandated**; the plan does not grade its own work. Acknowledge what was done well —
    61	accurate praise makes the rest of the feedback trusted.
    62	
    63	## Output
    64	
    65	Your final message IS the report — begin directly with the spec verdict; every line is a
    66	verdict, a finding with file:line, or a check you ran. No preamble, no closing summary.
    67	
    68	```
    69	### Spec Compliance
    70	✅ Spec compliant | ❌ Issues found: <missing/extra/misunderstood, file:line>
    71	⚠️ Cannot verify from diff: <items + what the controller should check>
    72	
    73	### Strengths
    74	<specific>
    75	
    76	### Issues
    77	#### Critical (Must Fix)
    78	#### Important (Should Fix)
    79	#### Minor (Nice to Have)
    80	<each: file:line, what's wrong, why it matters, how to fix if not obvious>
    81	
    82	### Assessment
    83	Task quality: Approved | Needs fixes
    84	Reasoning: <1-2 sentences>
    85	```

